\section{Where precision is lost}{


Four cases lose information, all of them because R's types are narrower than
Parquet's. Each is reported rather than silent.

\describe{
\item{\code{INT32} holding \code{-2147483648}}{R reserves that value as
\code{NA_integer_}, so it cannot be stored. It reads as \code{NA} with one warning
naming the column, however many values or batches were affected.
Promoting the column to \code{double} was rejected: \code{\link[=read_plan]{read_plan()}} is
a pure function of the schema, and a data-dependent type would let one
column arrive as different types in different batches.}
\item{64-bit integers past 2^53}{R's \code{double} is exact only within
\verb{[-2^53, 2^53]}. Values outside it read as \code{NA} with one warning naming
the column; pass \code{int64 = "integer64"} to keep the full signed range.}
\item{\code{DECIMAL}}{Read as \code{double} with the declared scale applied, which is
exact only while the unscaled integer stays within \verb{[-2^53, 2^53]}. Larger
precisions lose low-order digits. One message per read. Exact fixed-point
reads are planned for 0.2.0.}
\item{Sub-second timestamps far from the epoch}{\code{POSIXct} is a \code{double} of
seconds, so the further a timestamp is from 1970 the less sub-second
precision survives. A \code{NANOS} column shows this first.}
}

Writing loses nothing that reading would not: qio writes only the types it
can represent exactly. \code{POSIXct} is the one rounding case, to the declared
timestamp unit, which defaults to microseconds.
}

\section{What writing supports}{


Without a schema, qio infers: \code{logical} to \code{BOOLEAN}, \code{integer} to \code{INT32},
\code{double} to \code{DOUBLE}, \code{character} and \code{factor} to \code{BYTE_ARRAY} with \code{STRING},
\code{Date} to \code{INT32} with \code{DATE}, and \code{POSIXct} to \code{INT64} with a UTC-adjusted
\code{TIMESTAMP} in microseconds.

An explicit \code{\link[=parquet_schema]{parquet_schema()}} may additionally select \code{INT64} or \code{FLOAT} for
a numeric column, and a different \code{TIMESTAMP} unit. Those are the only
combinations the writer accepts; anything else is an error rather than a
silent fallback.

A column is written \code{OPTIONAL} when it contains any \code{NA} and \code{REQUIRED}
otherwise, except when appending, where the existing file decides. For
\code{double} columns \code{NA} becomes a Parquet null while \code{NaN} is preserved as a
value.
}

